   2d. (ADDED, verified) Not locale-sensitive: identical output under LC_ALL=C, POSIX, en_US.UTF-8 and tr_TR.UTF-8. Latin-1/Unicode space lookalikes are ordinary bytes: `/\xa0`, `/\xc2\xa0`, `/\x85`, `/\xa0a\xa0` all preserved verbatim.
   2e. (ADDED, verified) The no-trim property is independent of the rest of the URL: whitespace-padded segments survive unchanged with userinfo and port present (`http://u:p@e.com:80/ a / b ` -> 2: " a "," b "), with an empty host (`http:/// a /b`, `http://@/ a /b`, `http://:80/ a ` all keep " a "), with an IPv6 host (`http://[::1]/ a /b` -> 2: " a ","b"), with other schemes (`file://`, `ftp://`, `mailto:`), and when the whole string is the path because there is no `://` (`  a  /  b  ` -> 2: "  a  ","  b  "; `a/ ` -> 2: "a"," "; ` ` -> 1: " "; `\t` -> 1: "\t").

3. NO url_decode anywhere in this pipeline. "%41" stays "%41" (does NOT become "A"); "%2E%2E" stays "%2E%2E"; "%20" stays "%20"; "%09" stays "%09"; "%00" stays "%00" (so no NUL can be injected this way); "+" stays "+"; and critically "%2F" is NOT a separator: `/a%2Fb` -> 1 segment "a%2Fb", `/%2F` -> 1 segment "%2F". Percent-escapes are also not trimmed: `/ %20 / %09 ` -> 2 segments " %20 ", " %09 ".

4. NO dot-segment removal and NO dedup: `/./ /..` -> 3 [".", " ", ".."]; `/a/a/a/a` -> 4 ["a","a","a","a"].

5. Empty / all-separator inputs give 0 segments (LINE 5 prints "0", no LINE 6+): path "" -> 0; "/" -> 0; "//" -> 0; "///" -> 0; 1000 consecutive '/' -> 0.

6. Leading and trailing '/' contribute nothing; runs of '/' collapse, including runs adjacent to whitespace-only segments (`/a/ //b` -> 3 ["a"," ","b"], `/a// /b` -> 3 ["a"," ","b"]). Relative paths (no leading '/') behave identically: "a/b/c" -> 3; "/a/b/c" -> 3; "//a/b" -> 2; "a//b" -> 2; "/a//" -> 1.

7. Byte transparency: all high bytes 0x80..0xFF are preserved verbatim; no UTF-8 awareness or validation. `/\xc3\xa9\xff\x80/\xe4\xb8\xad` -> 2 segments with exactly those bytes (od -c confirmed). A 10 000-byte segment of repeated "\xc3\xa9" round-trips with an identical md5.

8. Output order is input order (first segment printed first). The count prints as a plain decimal integer with no locale grouping: 1000 -> "1000"; 5000 -> "5000"; 9999/10000/20000/30000 -> "9999"/"10000"/"20000"/"30000". A single 100 000-byte segment yields count 1 and is emitted in full. No length or count caps observed at any tested size (only the OS argv limit stopped larger probes).

9. What split_path receives is exactly URL::path(): the query (from the first '?') and fragment (from the first '#') are already removed by parse, and no whole-string trim happens either — `'  http://e.com/a  '` yields path "/a  " -> 1 segment "a  " (with scheme "  http" invalid and host "e.com" valid). This holds for every leading whitespace byte and combination (space, \t, \n, \r, \v, \f, and the run " \t\n\v\f\r"): the leading bytes are folded into the scheme, which then prints 0 on LINE 2, and the trailing bytes are folded into the last path segment. Stripping is order-independent in effect: `/a#b?c/d` and `/a?b#c/d` both give path "/a"; a '#' or '?' occurring before the "://" truncates the whole string first (`ht?tp://e.com/a/b` -> path "ht" -> 1 segment "ht"; `http://e.co#m/a/b` -> path "" -> 0 segments). Whitespace adjacent to the boundary stays on whichever side it falls: `/ ?` -> 1 segment " ", `/? ` -> 0 segments.
   9a. (ADDED, verified) The host is not trimmed either, and this is observable through LINE 3 together with the segments: `http://e.com   ` -> host "e.com   " (LINE 3 = 0), path "" -> 0 segments; `http://e.com   / a ` -> LINE 3 = 0, path "/ a " -> 1 segment " a "; `http://e.com a/b` -> LINE 3 = 0, 1 segment "b"; `http://[ ::1 ]/ a ` -> LINE 3 = 0, 1 segment " a ". So whitespace before the first '/' of the path stays in the authority and whitespace after it stays in the path; nothing is ever moved, merged, or removed by a trim.
